<p>U.S. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth faced nearly six hours of questioning Wednesday before the House Armed Services Committee, his first public congressional testimony since President Trump’s administration launched the war against Iran more than two months ago. Acting Pentagon comptroller Jules Hurst told lawmakers the conflict has cost about $25 billion so far, most of it for munitions, while Hegseth said the operation was needed to prevent Iran from obtaining a nuclear weapon. The hearing was scheduled to review the administration’s fiscal 2027 defense budget request of about $1.5 trillion, but lawmakers focused heavily on the Iran campaign, its legal basis, costs, weapons use and casualties. Democrats questioned the administration’s objectives and criticized the war as unauthorized by Congress; Republicans largely focused on military budgeting and voiced support for the operation. Hegseth rejected descriptions of the conflict as a “quagmire” and accused congressional Democrats and some Republicans of using “reckless, feckless and defeatist” language. Rep. Adam Smith, the committee’s top Democrat, pressed Hegseth on how U.S. strikes would produce gains against Iran’s nuclear program, while Rep. John Garamendi accused Hegseth of “lying to the American public about this war from day one” and said munitions use had depleted stocks needed to deter China.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/pg7Q1xt0kY6wr2kF_CCHU7WXuJmeHSdhWWgINh_1F_aJTC5A5NXCLz-UVla-xyirGZTSySzhOIctVSSWIvXkMI4jqRaF91hagtg0msVbGQ' alt='U.S. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth, Gen. Dan Caine and Pentagon official Jules Hurst testify before the House Armed Services Committee in Washington.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>War powers: The hearing took place two days before a 60-day deadline cited by lawmakers and aides for winding down hostilities under war-powers limits.</li><li>Casualty figures: Pentagon data cited by Navy Times said 13 American service members have been killed and 400 wounded in Operation Epic Fury.</li><li>Cost uncertainty: CNN reporting cited by KRDO said the $25 billion estimate did not include repairs for extensive damage to U.S. bases in the region, according to three people familiar with the matter.</li><li>Diplomacy status: President Trump rejected an Iranian proposal to reopen the Strait of Hormuz in exchange for lifting the U.S. blockade while delaying nuclear-program talks, according to AP live updates carried by NBC New York.</li><li>Next hearing: Hegseth was due to face senators on Thursday for a second day of questioning over the war.</li></ul><blockquote>The biggest adversary we face at this point are the reckless, feckless and defeatist words of congressional Democrats and some Republicans - Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth: Hegseth said the cost should be judged against the U.S. objective of preventing Iran from obtaining a nuclear weapon. (<a href='https://www.navytimes.com/news/pentagon-congress/2026/04/29/iran-war-has-cost-25-billion-so-far-pentagon-official-says/'>Navy Times</a>)</li><li>Rep. Adam Smith: Smith said President Trump’s claims that Iran had agreed to surrender its nuclear program and control of the Strait of Hormuz had not been borne out. “So wish fulfillment is not really a strategy,” he said. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/iran-war-25-billion-hegseth-congress/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Rep. John Garamendi: Garamendi called the conflict a “geopolitical calamity” and “strategic blunder,” saying it had caused a worldwide economic crisis and reduced U.S. munition levels below what he considered necessary to deter China. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1996210/pentagon-chief-spars-with-lawmakers-over-iran-war'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Gen. Dan Caine: Caine cited Gen. George C. Marshall’s commitment to a nonpartisan military as “a constant standard,” a statement made after Hegseth’s opening attack on war critics. (<a href='https://w